🔹 What is Serialization?

Serialization in Java = converting an object into a byte stream so it can be:

Stored (e.g. in a file or database)

Transmitted (e.g. over network, API, sockets, RMI)

Later deserialized back into the same object.

ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("user.ser"));
out.writeObject(user);   // Serialization

🔹 Benefits of Serialization

Persistence
Save objects to files/databases and restore them later.

Communication
Send objects over a network between different JVMs or microservices.

Caching
Store ready-made objects in cache for fast retrieval.

Deep Copy
Can be used to clone complex objects (via serialize/deserialize).

Distributed Systems
Essential for technologies like RMI, JMS, Kafka, Hibernate, etc.

🔹 Why do we use it?

Because normal objects exist only in JVM memory.
If the JVM stops → objects are gone.
Serialization allows us to save and share objects across JVM restarts or between systems.

✅ Interview Short Answer:
Serialization lets us convert an object into a byte stream so it can be saved or transferred.
 The main benefit is persistence and communication in distributed systems (e.g., saving to file, sending over network, caching).

 🔹 What is Deserialization?

 Deserialization is the reverse of serialization.
 It means converting a byte stream back into an object.

 👉 If you saved or sent an object using serialization, you can reconstruct the same object later with deserialization.

 🔹 Example in Java
 // Deserialization: read object back
 ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream("user.ser"));
 User user = (User) in.readObject();
 in.close();

 System.out.println("User: " + user);

 🔹 Why is it Useful?

 Retrieve stored objects from a file or DB.

 Receive objects sent across a network.

 Reconstruct session state (e.g., web servers).

 🔹 Key Point

 Serialization → Object → Byte Stream

 Deserialization → Byte Stream → Object

 ✅ Interview Short Answer:
 Deserialization is the process of converting a byte stream back into a Java object.
  It allows us to restore objects that were previously serialized and saved or transmitted.

